In this lesson we'll look at percentage error and figure out which error measure to use in different situations. Percentage error is really just the relative error written as a percentage, so you take the absolute error, divide it by the actual measurement, then multiply by one hundred. So which one should you actually use? If you're comparing two measurements on the same scale, like two lengths both in metres, stick with absolute error because it has the same units as your data and is easy to interpret. But the moment you're comparing across different scales or units, switch to percentage error. Think about it this way: a tiny error of one hundredth of a kilogram sounds harmless, but it actually matters a lot more on a one tenth of a kilogram bag of spices than on a one hundred kilogram sack of flour. Percentage error lets you fairly compare those situations. When you write up a measurement, the standard format is the value, plus or minus, the absolute error, and the decimal places of the value should match the decimal places of the error. Now let's see it in action.